{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787080221,"nonce":"a5+lFXl4D9usQEyyyFws+Jr9sxk=","title":"A digest policy over a quiet namespace warns every minute forever, and the count it reports as skipped windows is a backlog nothing was ever owed","message":"**What is wrong.** `sweepPolicy` advances the digest cursor only by writing a digest. A window that closes below the policy's floor writes nothing, so `LastWindow` does not move; the next tick therefore re-derives the same owed span one window longer. After `MaxDigestBackfill` quiet windows the span exceeds the cap permanently, and from then on **every tick logs a WARN whose `skipped_windows` count grows without bound**, on a policy that has never had anything to send.\n\n**The demonstration.** Three sites, and each one is individually correct. `sweepPolicy` (`internal/notification/service/digest.go:180-183`) reads the cursor from `s.digests.LastWindow`, which is the newest window with a stored digest row. `:222-227` then declines to store anything for a window that did not clear the floor, under a comment that names the consequence and calls it deliberate: *\"an unsent window advances no cursor, so a quiet stretch is re-examined by the next tick and then falls off the `MaxDigestBackfill` horizon. That is correct, because re-examining a closed window is a query whose answer cannot have changed.\"* And `DigestWindows` (`internal/notification/domain/digest.go:210-215`) computes `total` from `first = WindowStart(lastCovered) + Window` up to the newest closed window, and returns `abandoned = total - MaxDigestBackfill` whenever that exceeds six.\n\nPut them together on a policy with a 10-minute window over a namespace nobody has paged about since last Tuesday. `lastCovered` is last Tuesday. `newest` is now. `total` is thousands. `abandoned` is `total - 6` and rises by one every ten minutes, forever. The `notify.digest` tick runs every 60 s (SPEC §G.3), so `digest.go:192-197` fires a WARN on every one of them: `\"notification: digest windows skipped, too far behind\"` with `skipped_windows` in the thousands, `covered_windows: 6`, and `last_covered` frozen at a date last week. The six covered windows are then all below the floor, nothing is emitted, and the cursor does not move — so the next tick logs the same thing one higher.\n\n**Why it matters.** The log line is a data-loss report. Its own comment (`:184-189`) says what it exists to say: *\"⭐ THE SKIP IS SAID OUT LOUD, WHICH IS §B.6 APPLIED TO OTO'S OWN QUIET. `MaxDigestBackfill` deliberately drops the oldest owed windows rather than posting hours of stale summaries, and a damper that cannot report itself is the silent suppression oto refuses to ship.\"* That sentence is true of the case it was written for — a real outage, a real backlog of digests that were owed and will not be sent. It is false of this one, where nothing was owed and nothing was dropped, and the two are indistinguishable in the log. An operator who wires an alert on this message — which is exactly what a message shaped like this invites — gets paged by every quiet namespace they have. An operator who does not gets a permanently noisy log in which the one occurrence that means something is buried under thousands that do not.\n\nThere is also a real cost behind the noise: six `Buckets` queries per quiet policy per tick, forever, each one re-asking a question whose answer the previous tick already had.\n\n**Where.** `internal/notification/service/digest.go:175-183` (`sweepPolicy`, the cursor read), `:184-197` (the WARN and the comment that justifies it), `:222-236` (the below-floor `continue` and the comment that accepts the non-advancing cursor) · `internal/notification/domain/digest.go:144-172` (`MaxDigestBackfill` and the reasoning it rests on), `:186-221` (`DigestWindows`, where `abandoned` is computed) · `internal/notification/service/digest_tick_test.go:600-666` (the existing test, which asserts the WARN fires for a genuine backlog) · SPEC §G.3's `notify.digest` row (the 60 s tick).\n\n**Scope.** medium. The cheap fix — advance the cursor for a window that was examined and found below the floor — is a behaviour change on the audit surface, because \"no row for this window\" currently means two different things (not owed, and dropped) and a cursor that advances on both erases the second. It needs a way to distinguish \"covered, nothing to say\" from \"never covered\", which is either a separate cursor, a stored high-water mark on the policy, or a rule that `abandoned` is only reported when at least one covered window cleared. Whichever is chosen is a small change; choosing is the work.\n\n**Done when.** A digest policy over a namespace that has been quiet for a week logs nothing on a tick, and does no per-window query it does not need. `skipped_windows` counts windows that were genuinely owed a send and did not get one, never windows that were below the floor, so the message means what it says and is safe to alert on. A test covers the quiet-for-longer-than-`MaxDigestBackfill` case explicitly and asserts the log is silent — the mirror of the existing backfill test, which asserts it is not. Whatever mechanism advances past a quiet window leaves an unrecoverable gap distinguishable from a quiet stretch, because §B.6 requires oto's quiet to be inspectable and the current design uses the same absence for both.","files":null}]}